Changelog: 05-11-2026 13:49:03

Added. HD Controller: the first version, the BC250 suite's USB HD Control for this suite (owner, 05-11-2026), on the
portable window. Five cards: Controller (each DualSense and DualSense Edge Windows has, on USB or Bluetooth, read on
A: its firmware, its battery from its input reports, the report rate, and the spec sheet in the information; LB and
RB choose between two or more), Test (the drawing follows the controller live; A starts a test in which every button
only lights the drawing, until B is held for two seconds; X runs the left motor, then the right one; Y gives the
light bar its next color), Firmware (Y asks the official update server for the latest version, also when the app
opens, at most once a day; A downloads, checks and sends it over the USB cable, after a second press, with the Edge's
profiles backed up first; X sends a file of your own, picked in the suite's browser; the app does not close while it
runs), Profiles (the Edge's four, the active one marked; A creates or edits one on a page of rows, X clears one on a
second press, Y backs them up, LT reads them again; the default profile is never changed) and Settings (the check
when the app opens, and the introduction of three pages shown after the first controller is read). The protocol is
the BC250 app's (the firmware update, the profiles, the firmware report, the light bar); the app talks to the
controller through hidapi in place of Linux's hidraw, so Windows needs no rule, no driver and no administrator for it.
The motors get the output report hid-playstation sends, as Windows has no force feedback for the controller; over
Bluetooth the app reads the controller's calibration once, as Linux's driver does, so it sends its full reports. The
update refuses to start while Steam, DS4Windows or DSX runs. --info prints each controller's battery, firmware and
profiles without a window.
